From u0c39e60ddb38622b at groups.google.com  Tue Nov 15 09:53:47 2011
From: u0c39e60ddb38622b at groups.google.com (Dennis Murphy)
Date: Tue, 15 Nov 2011 09:53:47 +0000
Subject: Re: What does it mean "..scaled.."
Message-ID: <GIVO6j1tU5o.oWRodNkHpW0J@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <GIVO6j1tU5o.r1vSHeNqWewJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
References: <GIVO6j1tU5o.reOwvINYUMgJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com> <GIVO6j1tU5o.r1vSHeNqWewJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/GIVO6j1tU5o/m/oWRodNkHpW0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It means that the density is divided by its maximum so that when
rendered, the maximum value of the plotted density is one. It's also
referred to as a relative density. This idea is also applied to
likelihoods - relative likelihood is a likelihood function scaled so
that the maximum value of the likelihood is 1. So think of ..scaled..
as being something like
   ..scaled.. = ..density../max(..density..)

Dennis

